<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vardar Macedonia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yugoslav Macedonia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardar">Vardar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a> is the part of the wider region of Macedonia, around the Vardar valley, that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> won in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> of 1912–1913 and that belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> until 1991. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> occupied most of it in both world wars, hoping to annex it. In 1944 it became the People's Republic of Macedonia within Yugoslavia, and its territory now forms <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vardar-macedonia.jpg" alt="Vardar Macedonia" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author, but under the authority of the Socialist Republic of Macedonia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Socialist_Republic_of_Macedonia_(1946%E2%80%931991);_Flag_of_North_Macedonia_(1991-1992).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
